      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Run the simulator. Set the shortcut frequency to zero, then to 0.4. How much does final accuracy diverge between the two runs, even though both hit >90% on the training distribution?\n\n2. Add a held-out OOD test to the simulator. Draw problems from a different distribution and evaluate the bootstrapped model on both in-distribution and OOD sets. Quantify the gap.\n\n3. Read the Quiet-STaR paper (arXiv:2403.09629) Section 3. Explain the \"end-of-thought\" token and the mixing-weight head in three sentences each.\n\n4. Compare STaR's keep-if-correct filter to a process-supervised alternative that rewards each rationale step independently. Identify the labelling cost difference and the plausible quality difference.\n\n5. Design one evaluation that would catch shortcut rationales in a deployed model. It does not have to be perfect \u2014 it has to break the simplest shortcuts a STaR loop would reinforce."
      ]
    }
